/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Average Restaurant Ratings by City \(Top 20 Indian Cities\))
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (This bar chart illustrates the average restaurant ratings across the top 20 Indian cities in the Zomato dataset. The y-axis shows the average rating on a scale of 0 to 5, while the x-axis lists cities such as Chennai, Mumbai, Bangalore, Pune, Lucknow, Delhi, Jaipur, Ahmedabad, Kolkata, Amritsar, and more. Most cities report ratings in the range of 3.5 to 4.0, indicating a generally positive customer experience across major urban centers. Cities like Pune, Bangalore, and Amritsar stand out with higher average ratings, exceeding 4.0 in some cases, reflecting a strong dining culture and customer satisfaction. On the other hand, cities such as Aurangabad and Vellore record significantly lower averages, suggesting scope for improvement in restaurant quality and services. The visualization highlights regional variations in customer perceptions of dining experiences. Metropolitan cities such as Mumbai, Delhi, Bangalore, and Chennai consistently show above-average ratings, likely due to better infrastructure, more competitive restaurant markets, and diverse culinary options. Smaller cities, however, display more fluctuation, with some like Amritsar and Vijayawada performing surprisingly well, while others such as Aurangabad lag behind. These insights are valuable for both customers and businesses: customers can identify cities with the best-rated dining experiences, while restaurant owners and policymakers in underperforming areas can focus on improving quality, service, and variety to boost customer satisfaction.)
/E (This bar chart illustrates the average restaurant ratings across the top 20 Indian cities in the Zomato dataset. The y-axis shows the average rating on a scale of 0 to 5, while the x-axis lists cities such as Chennai, Mumbai, Bangalore, Pune, Lucknow, Delhi, Jaipur, Ahmedabad, Kolkata, Amritsar, and more. Most cities report ratings in the range of 3.5 to 4.0, indicating a generally positive customer experience across major urban centers. Cities like Pune, Bangalore, and Amritsar stand out with higher average ratings, exceeding 4.0 in some cases, reflecting a strong dining culture and customer satisfaction. On the other hand, cities such as Aurangabad and Vellore record significantly lower averages, suggesting scope for improvement in restaurant quality and services. The visualization highlights regional variations in customer perceptions of dining experiences. Metropolitan cities such as Mumbai, Delhi, Bangalore, and Chennai consistently show above-average ratings, likely due to better infrastructure, more competitive restaurant markets, and diverse culinary options. Smaller cities, however, display more fluctuation, with some like Amritsar and Vijayawada performing surprisingly well, while others such as Aurangabad lag behind. These insights are valuable for both customers and businesses: customers can identify cities with the best-rated dining experiences, while restaurant owners and policymakers in underperforming areas can focus on improving quality, service, and variety to boost customer satisfaction.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
