271 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (The bar chart illustrates the distribution of restaurants across various Indian cities, highlighting the dominance of a few urban centers. Chennai leads with the highest number of restaurants, followed by Mumbai, Bangalore, and Pune, which together form the major hubs of food services in India. Other cities like Lucknow, Kolkata, New Delhi, and Jaipur also show significant restaurant presence, though comparatively smaller in scale. Beyond the top metros, the number of restaurants gradually declines across tier-2 and tier-3 cities, reflecting the concentration of Zomato-listed outlets in metropolitan and highly populated regions. This visualization clearly shows the uneven distribution, where a few cities contribute disproportionately to the overall restaurant count, while smaller towns and suburban areas remain underrepresented.)
/E (The bar chart illustrates the distribution of restaurants across various Indian cities, highlighting the dominance of a few urban centers. Chennai leads with the highest number of restaurants, followed by Mumbai, Bangalore, and Pune, which together form the major hubs of food services in India. Other cities like Lucknow, Kolkata, New Delhi, and Jaipur also show significant restaurant presence, though comparatively smaller in scale. Beyond the top metros, the number of restaurants gradually declines across tier-2 and tier-3 cities, reflecting the concentration of Zomato-listed outlets in metropolitan and highly populated regions. This visualization clearly shows the uneven distribution, where a few cities contribute disproportionately to the overall restaurant count, while smaller towns and suburban areas remain underrepresented.)
